1 NOTE {geni:about_me} *[http://www.ChasamSofer.com '''Chasam Sofer website''']
2 CONT *[http://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Chatam_Sofer  '''Wikipedia bio''']
2 CONT *[http://www.loebtree.com/kalischer.html#sofer '''Chatam Sofer family tree''']
2 CONT *[http://www.chasamsofer.com/wp-content/uploads/2012/08/%D7%9E%D7%A7%D7%95%D7%9D-%D7%9E%D7%A0%D7%95%D7%97%D7%AA-%D7%91%D7%A0%D7%99-%D7%94%D7%97%D7%AA%D7%A1.pdf '''מקום מנוחת בני החת"ס''']
2 CONT
2 CONT [http://www.ChasamSofer.com  '''Rabbi Moshe Sofer'''], (German: Moses Schreiber), also known by his main work Chatam (in Yiddish:  "Chasam") Sofer, (trans. Seal of the Scribe), (1762 - 1839), was one of the leading rabbis of Europ
2 CONC ean Jewry in the first half of the nineteenth century his influence is still felt today. He is also the patriarch of the Sofer family of rabbis.
2 CONT
2 CONT [http://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Chatam_Sofer '''Moshe Sofer'''] was born in Frankfurt am Main, (now Germany) on September 24, 1762, during the Seven Years' War. (8 Tishrei 5523 on the Hebrew calendar).
2 CONT
2 CONT His father's name was Shmuel (Samuel) (d. 1779, 15 Sivan 5539) and his mother's name was Reisil the daughter of Elchanan (d. 1822, 17 Adar 5582).
2 CONT
2 CONT Shmuel's mother Reizchen (d. 5 May 1731 in Frankfurt am Main) was a daughter of the Gaon of Frankfurt Rabbi Shmuel Schotten, known as the Marsheishoch (died, 1719, 14 Tamuz 5479 in Frankfurt am Main), his namesake.
2 CONT
2 CONT '''''Biography''''':
2 CONT
2 CONT '''Education'''
2 CONT
2 CONT At the age of nine Moshe entered the yeshiva of Rabbi Nathan Adler (1742-1800, d. 27 Elul 5560) at Frankfurt, and when only thirteen years old he delivered public lectures. He was so extraordinary that Rabbi Pinchas Horowitz of Fr
2 CONC ankfurt asked him to become his pupil. He agreed, but remained under Rabbi Horowitz for only one year, and then left in 1776 for the yeshiva of Rabbi David Tebele Scheuer (1712-1782, d. Shmini Atzeres 5543) in the neighboring city
2 CONC  of Mainz, which gladly welcomed him. There he studied under its Rosh Yeshiva Rabbi Mechel Scheuer (1739-1810 d. 27 Shevat 5570) son of Rabbi Tebele during the years 1776 and 1777 until he yielded to the entreaties of his former t
2 CONC eachers in Frankfurt and returned to his native city. In Mainz many prominent residents took an interest in his welfare and facilitated the progress of his studies. In addition to his vast Talmudic knowledge, he was also proficien
2 CONC t in astronomy, geometry, and history.
2 CONT
2 CONT '''Boskowitz, Prossnitz, Dresnitz, and Mattersdorf'''
2 CONT
2 CONT In 1782 Rabbi Nathan Adler (see profile https://www.geni.com/people/Rabbi-Nathan-Adler-Ha-Chasid/6000000011048675439 ) was called to the rabbinate of Boskowitz (Boskovice), Moravia, Austrian Habsburg (now Boskovice, Czech Republic
2 CONC ) and Rabbi Sofer followed him. He went, at Rabbi Adler's advice, to Prossnitz (Prostejov), where he married Sarah (d. 1812), the daughter of the deceased rabbi of Prossnitz, Rabbi Moses Jerwitz (d. 1785). Rabbi Moses Sofer became
2 CONC  a member of the Chevra Kadisha (Shu"t Chasam Sofer, Y"D:327) and eventually became head of the yeshiva there.
2 CONT
2 CONT In 1794, Rabbi Sofer accepted his first official position, becoming Rabbi of Dresnitz , after he had procured the sanction of the government to settle in that town. In 1797 he was appointed Rabbi of Mattersdorf (currently Mattersb
2 CONC urg, Austria); one of the seven communities (known as the Sheva kehillot) of Burgenland. There he established a yeshiva, and pupils flocked to him. His prime pupil in Mattersdorf, was the future Gaon Rabbi Meir Ash (Maharam Ash) (
2 CONC 1780-1854), Rabbi of Ungvar.
2 CONT
2 CONT '''Work in Pressburg (today's Bratislava) and his death'''
2 CONT
2 CONT He declined many offers for the rabbinate, but in 1806 accepted a call to Pressburg, Austrian Empire (now Bratislava, capital of Slovakia). In Pressburg, he established a yeshiva, which was attended by as many as 500 pupils. Hundr
2 CONC eds of these pupils became the rabbis of Hungarian Jewry. Among them were:
2 CONT *https://www.geni.com/people/Avraham-Shaag-Zwebner/6000000000653070056, (1801-1876)
2 CONT *https://www.geni.com/people/R-Moshe-Schick-The-MaHaRaM-Schick-%D7%9E%D7%94%D7%A8-%D7%9D-%D7%A9%D7%99%D7%A7/6000000003164340669, (1807-1879)
2 CONT *https://www.geni.com/people/Rabbi-David-Schick-Imrei-David-A-B-D-Tokaj/6000000001768939031 (died: 1890) brother of Moshe Schick
2 CONT *https://www.geni.com/people/Chaim-Zvi-Mannheimer-Rabbi-of-Verbau-Ungvar/6000000020037226489, (1814-1886)
2 CONT *https://www.geni.com/people/Hillel-Lichtenstein-Kolemayer/6000000002765192694, (1815-1891)
2 CONT *https://www.geni.com/people/Reb-Avraham-Yehuda-HaKohen-Schwartz-Kol-Aryeh/6000000006708386715, (1824-1875)
2 CONT *https://www.geni.com/people/Rabbi-Meir-Perls-of-Krola/6000000058618966089, (1811-1893)
2 CONT *https://www.geni.com/people/Rabbi-Chaim-Sofer-%D7%91%D7%A2%D7%9C-%D7%9E%D7%97%D7%A0%D7%94-%D7%97%D7%99%D7%99%D7%9D/6000000002764369626, (1822-1886)
2 CONT *https://www.geni.com/people/Menachem-Paneth-Maglei-Tzedek/6000000000224920837, (1818-1884)
2 CONT *https://www.geni.com/people/HaGaon-Rav-Menachem-Katz-Prostits/6000000016205381341, (1795-1891)
2 CONT *https://www.geni.com/people/Aron-Singer/6000000011610632574, (c. 1806-1868)
2 CONT *https://www.geni.com/people/R-Shimon-Sidon/5136844574920045843(1815 - 1891), Rabbi of Cifer and Trnava
2 CONT *https://www.geni.com/people/Yisrael-Yitzchak-Aharon-Landesberg/6000000075152175852, (1804-1879)
2 CONT *https://www.geni.com/people/Aron-Fried-A-B-D-Hajduboszorneny-Besamin/5604725357040069591, (1813-1891)
2 CONT *https://www.geni.com/people/Aaron-Deutsch-Goren-David/6000000007227160468?tracking_source=records, (1813-1878)
2 CONT *https://www.geni.com/people/Yehuda-Modrin/6000000002764792173, (1820-1893)
2 CONT *Rabbi Yoel Unger, (1800-1886)
2 CONT *https://www.geni.com/people/Naftali-Sofer-auth-Mate-Naftali/6000000002627189429, (1819-1899)
2 CONT *https://www.geni.com/people/Avraham-Sofer/6000000001398264758?through=6000000001398290180, (1815-1872) (son)
2 CONT *https://www.geni.com/people/Shimon-Schreiber-Sofer-Michtav-Sofer/6000000001343854209?through=6000000001398264758, (1821-1883) (son)
2 CONT *https://www.geni.com/people/David-Zvi-Ehrenfeld-Rav/6000000007257894762 (c.1809-1861), (son-in-law)
2 CONT * https://www.geni.com/people/R-Samuel-Ehrenfeld/6000000000034924837?through=6000000007257894762(1835-1883), (Chasan Sofer) (grandson)
2 CONT *https://www.geni.com/people/Yehuda-Aszod-Yehudah-Ya-aleh/6000000005406488269 ( -1866)
2 CONT *https://www.geni.com/people/Binyumin-Shlomo-Spitzer-Tikkun-Shlomo/6000000006712194675 ,  (1811-1893), (son-in-law), Rabbi of Schiff Shul in Vienna
2 CONT *https://www.geni.com/people/Akiva-Schlesinger-A-B-D-Bratislava/6000000000739429001 ,(1838-1922),
2 CONT
2 CONT His students also numbered many rabbinic leaders, including https://www.geni.com/people/R-Chaim-Yosef-Gottlieb-A-B-D-Stropkov/6000000003410159383.
2 CONT
2 CONT This is a project aimed at collecting all his students; https://www.geni.com/projects/Talmidim-of-Chasam-Sofer-and-his-family/people/56300
2 CONT
2 CONT [http://www.loebtree.com/kalischer.html#sofer '''Chatam Sofer'''] was married twice. His first wife was Malka Yarevits (Jerwitz) - died childless. He then [in 1812 (23 Cheshven 5573)] married Sarel (Sarah) (1790-1832, d. 18 Adar I
2 CONC I 5592), the widowed daughter of Rabbi Akiba Eiger, (1761-1837) Rabbi of Posen. She was the widow of Rabbi Avraham Moshe Kalischer (1788-1812), Rabbi of Piła, the son of Rabbi Yehuda Kalischer, author of "Hayad Hachazaka". Rabbi M
2 CONC oshe and Sarah had 11 children (5 sons and 6 daughters).
2 CONT
2 CONT The Chatam Sofer died in Pressburg on October 3, 1839 (25 Tishrei 5600), and was succeeded by his son Rabbi Avraham Shmuel Binyamin Sofer known as the Ketav Sofer.
2 CONT
2 CONT '''''Fight against changes in Judaism'''''
2 CONT
2 CONT '''The Chasam Sofer led the fight against the Reform Movement'''
2 CONT
2 CONT From the late 18th century onwards, movements which eventually developed into Reform Judaism began to progress. Synagogues subscribing to these new views began to appear in centres such as Berlin and Hamburg. Rabbi Sofer was profo
2 CONC undly opposed to the reformers and attacked them in his speeches and writings. For example in a responsum of 1816 he forbade the congregation in Vienna to allow a performance in the synagogue of a cantata they had commissioned fro
2 CONC m the composer Ignaz Moscheles because it would involve a mixed choir. In the same spirit he also contested the founders of the Reformschule (Reform synagogue) in Pressburg, which was established in the year 1827.
2 CONT
2 CONT In response to those who stated that Judaism could change or evolve, Rabbi Sofer applied the motto Hadash asur min ha-Torah (חדש אסור מן התורה), "Anything new is forbidden by the Torah," (homelitically based on the Biblical law, i
2 CONC n Leviticus 23:14, that new grains are forbidden to be used before Passover, see Yoshon). For Rabbi Sofer, Judaism as previously practiced was the only form of Judaism acceptable. In his view the rules and tenets of Judaism never 
2 CONC changed — and cannot ever change. This became the defining idea for the opponents to Reform, and in some form, it has continued to influence Orthodox response to innovation in Jewish doctrine and practice.
2 CONT
2 CONT '''Influence'''
2 CONT
2 CONT He is one of the most important figures in the development of Haredi Judaism, where his influence is still felt today. He is also the patriarch of the Sofer family of rabbis. His son Rabbi Avrohom Shmuel Binyamin Sofer (1815-1872)
2 CONC  (the Ktav Sofer) succeeded him as rabbi of Pressburg, and his son Rabbi Shimon Sofer (1821-1883) became rabbi of Kraków. One of his descendants is the Grand Rabbi of the Erlau sect, which is a Hasidic-style sect in Jerusalem that
2 CONC  follows the customs of Rabbi Moses Sofer, as opposed to Hasidic customs of prayer.
2 CONT
2 CONT In Orthodox Jewry, he is an often-quoted authority. Many of his responsa are required reading for semicha (rabbinic ordination); his novellæ on the Torah sparked a new style in Torah commentary, and some editions of the Talmud con
2 CONC tain his emendations and additions.
2 CONT
2 CONT His students included many rabbinic leaders, including Rabbi Hayyim Joseph Gottlieb of Stropkov.
2 CONT
2 CONT A modern Jewish memorial, containing Moses Sofer's grave and those of many of his associates and family is in Bratislava. It is situated underground below Bratislava Castle at the left bank of the Danube). The nearby tram station 
2 CONC is named after him.
2 CONT
2 CONT See also: [http://wiki.geni.com/index.php/Jewish_Dynasties '''''Jewish Dynasties''''']
2 CONT
2 CONT 1913--1996- The Eger Family Association- pg.8,20
2 CONT
2 CONT 1913--1990- The Eger Family Association אילן ז, אילן זז,
2 CONT --------------------
2 CONT
2 CONT www.ChasamSofer.com http://www.tora.co.il/gdolim/chatam.htm
2 CONT --------------------
2 CONT There are several books which speak about The Chatam/Hatam Sofer's life, including Patai's autobiographic work about Patai growing up in Budapest.  In this book he traces his lineage to the Chatam Sofer and to Rabbi Akiva Eger and
2 CONC  describes aspect of their lives, giving interesting details for each one.  (Y.M., 7/30/14)
2 CONT --------------------
2 CONT Moses Schreiber (1762–1839), known to his own community and Jewish posterity in the Hebrew translation as Moshe Sofer, also known by his main work Chatam Sofer, Chasam Sofer or Hatam Sofer, (trans. Seal of the Scribe and acronym f
2 CONC or Chiddushei Torat Moshe Sofer), was one of the leading Orthodox rabbis of European Jewry in the first half of the nineteenth century. He was a teacher to thousands and a powerful opponent to the Reform movement in Judaism, which
2 CONC  was attracting many people from the Jewish communities in Austria-Hungary and beyond. As Rav of the city of Pressburg, he maintained a strong Orthodox Jewish perspective through communal life, first-class education, and uncomprom
2 CONC ising opposition to Reform and radical change. Sofer established a yeshiva in Bratislava (Pressburg in German), the Pressburg Yeshiva, which became the most influential yeshiva in Central Europe, producing hundreds of future leade
2 CONC rs of Hungarian Jewry. This yeshiva continued to function until World War II; afterward, it was relocated to Jerusalem under the leadership of the Chasam Sofer's great-grandson, Rabbi Akiva Sofer (the Daas Sofer).
