1 NOTE {geni:about_me} === GEDCOM Note ===
2 CONT Goudsmit eler Goldschmidt https://goldlog.goudsmit.amsterdam
2 CONT
2 CONT Goudsmit Goldschmidt & related Generations — in 5 centuries: the years from 1400 up to 1900
2 CONT
2 CONT An extensive chain of Goudsmit and Goldschmidt namesakes and their cognate families. Generations linked and followed in time and place — there and back from the nineteenth to the fifteenth century, from the Netherlands to Switzerl
2 CONC and. Over 33.000 descendants and relatives of Moshe bar Joseph segal — usually named Mostel von Schaffhausen — who in 1461 had become a rightful resident of Nürnberg. There he acquired the exceptional privilege to practice as a Go
2 CONC ldsmith, as well for his sons to learn the craft. In the Council books of 4 decades their names are regularly mentioned — until 1499, when all Jews were expelled from Nürnberg. Moshe and his family wandered from town to town till 
2 CONC about 1515, as he was admitted in Frankfurt-am-Main. He settled in one of the first houses of the street that was allocated apartly as the “Judengasse”. This house, named “goldener Schwan”, remained during centuries lived in by re
2 CONC latives of the ‘Goldschmidt’ family, while its expanding generations were compelled to move into other houses or even to find a home elsewhere. One of Mostel’s grandchildren Juspa bar Isaak had become a goldsmith of great fame and
2 CONC  even achieved the esteem of emperor Karl V, who by personal intercession in 1547 ordered that he was granted the special permission to build his own house. In 1614 the street was plundered and all Jews were expelled from Frankfur
2 CONC t. Within two years the instigator, guild-leader Fettmilch, and his accomplices were removed and sentenced. And under direct protection of Emperor Matthias the Jews were invited to return. Not all families did so, but still the Ju
2 CONC dengasse got severely overcrowded, within the period of only a few generations. Later in the 17th century two ‘Goldschmidt’ descendants had settled back to Frankfurt. Meyer son of Benedikt moved in from Kassel and married into the
2 CONC  house “Buchsbaum”. His nephew Isaak son of Jobst, coming via Hameln, obtained a new right of residence to live in the House “Korb und Wanne”. Both had numerous offspring. Their branches were usually discerned by the Council Tax A
2 CONC dministration with the surnames Goldschmidt-Kassel and Goldschmidt-Hameln. In 1711 a disastrous fire destroyed the Judengasse. All houses were lost. During the rebuilding everybody had to live in the region; mostly temporarily, bu
2 CONC t some families started wandering again and did not return. Ten years later once more a great fire destroyed half of the Ghetto. Those who in spite of all came back again end stayed in Frankfurt, kept in touch with their families 
2 CONC far away. During the centuries several Goldschmidt branches had dispersed over other towns: Stuttgart, Hildesheim, Stadthagen, Hannover, Hamburg, Oldenburg, Lemgo…. and some moved abroad to countries like the Netherlands, to Amste
2 CONC rdam or Rotterdam, and to provinces like Groningen, Overijssel or Noord-Brabant. And in the language of the new homeland their surname got changed — by translation into Goudsmit. 
2 CONT
2 CONT --------------------
