1 OCCU Artist
1 NOTE {geni:about_me} https://es.wikipedia.org/wiki/Ruth_Cahn
2 CONT
2 CONT https://de.wikipedia.org/wiki/Ruth_Cahn
2 CONT
2 CONT Amalie Leontine Cahn was born in Frankfurt's Myliusstraße as the only daughter of a long-established Frankfurt Jewish family. Their history can be traced back to the "Schwarzer Adler" house in Frankfurt's Judengasse ; their earlie
2 CONC st ancestors were admitted to the Frankfurt office in 1505. Her father was a consultant at the Chamber of Commerce and successfully ran a bank named after him on Taunusstraße. Her younger brothers Robert (1878-1948) and Arthur (18
2 CONC 83-1952) established themselves as entrepreneurs early on.
2 CONT
2 CONT From 1906 to 1912 Ruth Cahn attended the women's academy of the Munich Artists' Association and studied there with Max Feldbauer . Up to the end of the First World War, apart from Frankfurt, she stayed mainly in Spain, which was n
2 CONC eutral at the time, and where the consequences of the economic crisis and the war were felt less drastically than in Germany or France.
2 CONT
2 CONT In the early 1920s, Cahn lived and worked on rue Corneille and rue Saint-Jacques, respectively, on the edge of the Montparnasse quarter in Paris, where her brother Robert was already staying. Here she was in the circles of Pablo P
2 CONC icasso and Henri Matisse , with whom she is said to have been personally acquainted. In this creative environment she continued her artistic education with Kees van Dongen and Othon Friesz . Both were by then already celebrated ce
2 CONC lebrities within European art and known as the "Fauves".
2 CONT
2 CONT In addition to her membership in the Frankfurter Künstlerbund, in whose autumn and spring exhibitions she regularly took part for more than ten years, Ruth Cahn was a member of GEDOK , which was founded in 1926 as a community of G
2 CONC erman and Austrian artists and art lovers.
2 CONT
2 CONT In 1935 the painter was forced to leave Germany due to Nazi persecution. She went into exile in Chile with her brother . There is little evidence of the further stages of her life. What is certain is that she gave up painting and,
2 CONC  although she only died at the age of 90, never returned to the easel.
2 CONT
2 CONT After the death of her brother Arthur, Ruth Cahn returned to Europe. By the end of 1953, by 1954 at the latest, her move to Barcelona was complete. For the last five years of her life until her death in 1966, Ruth Cahn moved into 
2 CONC an apartment in Frankfurt's Westend. The painter, who was once one of the most respected artists in the city, was buried in the New Jewish Cemetery in Frankfurt.
