1 NAME Wilhelm Bernhard /Bonn/
2 GIVN Wilhelm Bernhard
2 SURN Bonn
2 _MARNM Bonn
1 SEX M
1 BIRT
2 DATE 16 MAR 1843
2 PLAC Frankfurt Am Main,Hessen-Nassau,Preussen
2 ADDR
1 DEAT
2 DATE 21 OCT 1910
2 ADDR
3 CITY Kronberg Im Taunus
3 STAE HE
3 CTRY Germany
1 OCCU Bankier
1 NOTE {geni:about_me} https://www.hohenemsgenealogie.at/gen/getperson.php?personID=I12696
2 CONT
2 CONT Wilhelm Bernhard Bonn was born on March 16, 1843 in Frankfurt am Main. He was the sixth of ten children of Baruch Bonn and his wife Betty, who came from the equally important and very wealthy Frankfurt Schuster family.
2 CONT Bonn was one of the prominent private bankers in Frankfurt and ended up being one of the 300 millionaires in the city. He came from the twelfth generation of a family that had come to Frankfurt from Spain around 1520 via the Rhine
2 CONC  Valley. As was customary at the time, the progenitor Jakob was given the family name Bonn zum Hirsch by the authorities after his previous residence and the name of his house in the Frankfurt ghetto.
2 CONT Over the centuries, the Bonns then operated as more or less successful merchants, money dealers and financiers. Over time, three Bonns were leaders of the Jewish community, one of the largest in Germany. Bonn's grandfather already
2 CONC  owned eleven houses. His father Baruch Bonn (1810–1878) turned his manufacturing goods store into the Bankhaus Baruch Bonn, which was managed by two sons for many years, but was taken over by the Palatinate Bank in 1920.
2 CONT After graduating from Realschule Philanthropin, Wilhelm Bernhard Bonn trained as a bank clerk at the Lazard Speyer-Ellissen bank. Because of his good knowledge of English and his business talent, the nineteen-year-old was sent to 
2 CONC the subsidiary bank Speyer & Co. in New York in 1863. The main focus there was on financing railroad projects such as the Union Pacific and the Central & Southern Pacific Railroads, a business in which especially Frankfurt banks w
2 CONC ere involved.
2 CONT Bonn was so successful that in 1866 he became managing director of the New Yorker Bank and remained so until his return to Germany in 1885. At the same time he founded his own bank, Ruette & Bonn, which also became active in Londo
2 CONC n with his brother Leopold as a partner. After returning from America, Bonn became a shareholder in the parent company Lazard Speyer-Ellissen until he left in 1906. The family connections of the shareholders made the bank one of t
2 CONC he largest private banks in Frankfurt. Its headquarters had been on the corner of Taunusanlage and Mainzer Landstrasse since 1900, where the black office building erected by Chase Manhattan Bank now stands.
2 CONT Increasing competition, World War II, the economic crisis and finally the seizure of power by the National Socialists in 1933 led to the liquidation of the bank in 1934. The beautiful Gründerzeit bank building survived the Second 
2 CONC World War, but was demolished in 1969 as part of Frankfurt's modernization policy. With the millions he had acquired in America, Wilhelm Bernhard Bonn also became one of the great benefactors in Frankfurt, similar to Charles Hallg
2 CONC arten, Jakob Schiff and Henry Budge, who had also become rich in America.
2 CONT Bonn supported more than two dozen foundations and institutions. He is one of the "permanent members" of the Senckenberg Natural Research Society and the Physical Society. The Freies Deutsches Hochstift was just as important to hi
2 CONC m as the then young university, the Goldschmidtsche Scholarship Foundation and Dr. Christ's Children's Hospital, to name just a few.
2 CONT The history of the Bonns in Germany ended with Wilhelm Bernhard Bonn. His first wife had died in New York. His son Max from his first marriage joined the Ruette & Bonn banking house in London, managed by Bonn's brother Leopold, at
2 CONC  an early age. After the death of his father in 1910, Max became head of the Bonn family and died in 1943 as Sir Max Bonn in London. Leopold Bonn, who at the same time became a partner at Speyer Brothers and a member of the board 
2 CONC of directors of the German Bank of London, died in England as a respected financial expert. Bonn's daughter Emma Bonn lived unmarried and wrote in a villa in Feldafing until she was taken to the Theresienstadt concentration camp b
2 CONC y the Nazis in 1942 and died there.
2 CONT Bonn's second wife Amelie had the Kronberg summer house sold to the city of Kronberg in 1922 and in 1923 she sold the Villa Bonn, the social meeting place of the extensive family, to the Frankfurt Society for Trade, Industry and S
2 CONC cience, which has preserved the monument to this day.
2 CONT At his funeral on October 25, 1910 at the Jewish cemetery on Rat-Beil-Strasse in Frankfurt, an "extremely numerous mourners" gathered, the "Frankfurter Zeitung" reported. Leading the way was the legendary Mayor of Frankfurt, Franz
2 CONC  Adickes. Also represented was the mayor of Kronberg, who made Bonn an honorary citizen and named a street after him. It was thanks for his generosity, because he had given the Taunus community an aid organization, the "Foundation
2 CONC  Baruch and Betty Bonn'sches Vorsorgewerk der Gemeinde Cronberg".
2 CONT Among the mourners were numerous representatives of foundations, cultural and social institutions from Bonn
2 CONT
2 CONT
2 CONT
2 CONT
2 CONT
2 CONT
2 CONT
2 CONT
