1 NAME Hermann /Schwab/
2 GIVN Hermann
2 _MARNM Schwab
1 SEX M
1 BIRT
2 DATE 7 APR 1879
2 PLAC Frankfurt am Main, Hessen-Nassau, German Empire
2 ADDR
1 DEAT
2 DATE 1 JUL 1962
2 PLAC London, England
2 ADDR
1 OCCU Journalist
1 NOTE {geni:about_me} https://www.lagis-hessen.de/pnd/127932232
2 CONT https://de.wikipedia.org/wiki/Hermann_Schwab_(Journalist)
2 CONT
2 CONT SCHWAB, HERMANN (1879–1962), German journalist and historical writer. In his native Frankfurt Schwab attended the Realschule of the Israelitische Religionsgesellschaft. He was employed from 1902 to 1927 in the metal enterprises of
2 CONC  Aron Hirsch near Berlin and in Halberstadt. He also worked as a journalist for the Frankfurter Zeitung, the Vossische Zeitung and other newspapers, was editor of the Juedischer Volksschriftenverlag, and author of Orthodox childre
2 CONC n's books. A supporter of *Agudat Israel and its founder, Jacob *Rosenheim, Schwab organized its war orphans' fund during World War i, which established a number of orphanages in Poland. From 1927 he ran a press service in Berlin 
2 CONC which he continued after emigrating to England in 1934. In London, he became president of the Golders Green Beth Hamidrash.
2 CONT
2 CONT His main published works are: Kindertraeume: Ein Maerchenbuch fuer juedische Kinder (1911; Dreams of Childhood, 1945); Orthodoxie und Zionismus… (1919); Aus der Schuetzenstrasse (1923; Memories of Frankfurt, 1955); A World in Ruin
2 CONC s: History, Life and Work of German Jewry (1946); The History of Orthodox Jewry in Germany (1950); 1933: Ein Tagebuch (1953); Jewish Rural Communities in Germany (1956); and Chachme Ashkenaz (Eng., 1964).
2 CONT
2 CONT bibliography: E.S. Schwab (ed.), Hermann Schwab (1963); A. Carlebach, "Hermann Schwab," in: lbi Yearbook (1962); P. Arnsberg, Die Geschichte der Frankfurter Juden… (1983), vol. 3, 477–79.
2 CONT
2 CONT === GEDCOM Note ===
2 CONT Stichworte zu: 0 Kommentare  Schwab, Hermann Spiegelstraße 57   Im Stadtplan anzeigen
