3 CTRY Ireland
1 NOTE {geni:about_me} She has been called '"the most important Jewish woman in Irish history"
2 CONT
2 CONT https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Ellen_Cuffe,_Countess_of_Desart
2 CONT
2 CONT https://www.findagrave.com/memorial/26535336/ellen_odette_cuffe
2 CONT Ellen Odette “Countess of Desart” Bischoffsheim Cuffe Famous memorial
2 CONT BIRTH     1 Sep 1857, London, City of London, Greater London, England
2 CONT DEATH    29 Jun 1933 (aged 75), Dublin, County Dublin, Ireland
2 CONT BURIAL    Falmouth Cemetery, Falmouth, Cornwall Unitary Authority, Cornwall, England
2 CONT PLOT        Sec. E. Row B. Grave 2, 
2 CONT MEMORIAL ID 26535336 · View Source
2 CONT British (Irish) Senator. Ellen Odette Bischoffsheim was born in London, the second daughter of Henry Louis Bischoffsheim, a wealthy banker, and his wife Clarisse (nee Bledermann.) She was educated at home. On the 29th. April 1881,
2 CONC  at Christ Church in Down Street, Mayfair, she married the Fourth Earl of Desart. He died in 1898 and, although she spent most of the next decade and a half in England, in 1912, she moved to County Kilkenny in Ireland. In her entr
2 CONC y in "Who's Who", she described her interests as: "The usual duties of a well-educated intelligent woman, conscientiously carried out; very strong anti-suffrage views; took an active part in England in combating the agitation for 
2 CONC women's suffrage." Nonetheless, in 1922, she was nominated by President Cosgrave for membership of the Senate of the Irish Free State, and accepted the position, becoming the first Jewish woman to be a member of any Senate in the 
2 CONC world. Another of her interests was the Irish language. She never learned to speak it herself, but funded schools to teach it, believing that there were parallels between the Gaelic and the Hebrew tongues, as both had become almos
2 CONC t obsolete but had been revived. She wrote only two books: "A Guide for Secretaries, Public and Private", and "Style and Title", but contributed many articles for publication in periodicals. She died in Waterloo Road in Dublin, bu
2 CONC t was buried next to her husband in Falmouth, where her funeral service was conducted according to the Jewish rites.
2 CONT Bio by: Iain MacFarlaine
