1 OCCU Kaufmann
1 NOTE {geni:about_me} Leon Hackenbroch was my grandfather. He was born in 1876 and passed away in 1952. I recall him vividly although I was a small child because I lived with my grandparents during the week for two years in order to att
2 CONC end a school of my parents' choice (we lived quite a distance away). I only realized fairly recently how much he epitomized "Torah Im Derekh Eretz." He would rise early and immediately rinse his hand with nagel wasser, was very or
2 CONC thodox but loved classical music and could quote Goethe and Schiller by the book. He adored my grandmother Agatha who was a most renowned beauty. He was temperamental on occasions but my grandma knew exactly how to handle things! 
2 CONC He was a jeweller by profession. One day he asked me if I would like to see something from his "jewellery case" and dangled a necklace of rubies casually. But I was only five and imagined that rubies should be of a deep red althou
2 CONC gh of course didn't say so. He liked us children to kiss his hand in the old Victorian style which I found very strange.
2 CONT
2 CONT He told me wonderful folk tales. Going for a daily walk with him while we walked my grandparents's dog was a riot, usually winding up with the dog's lead round the nearest lampost with us in tow.Above all he was a man of impeccabl
2 CONC e morals in business and appearance and rejected the new post war world business dealings in jewellery. So you see I grew up partly influenced by a Victorian gentleman from the old world.  And then of course there was my wonderful
2 CONC , beautiful grandmama.
2 CONT
2 CONT Contributed by their loving grand-daughter Ida Esther Koschland nee Hackenbroch
2 CONT
2 CONT
