0 @I6000000025510773830@ INDI
1 NAME Frank J. /Schwarzadler/
2 GIVN Frank J.
2 SURN Schwarzadler
2 _MARNM Adler
2 NICK Schwarz-Adler
1 SEX M
1 BIRT
2 DATE 27 OCT 1921
2 ADDR
3 CITY Frankfurt
3 STAE Hesse
3 CTRY Germany
1 DEAT
2 DATE 30 MAR 2018
2 ADDR
3 CITY Lawrence
3 STAE Kansas
3 CTRY United States
1 BURI
2 DATE 2 APR 2018
2 PLAC Rose Hill Cemetery
2 ADDR
3 CITY Kansas City
3 STAE Missouri
3 CTRY United States
1 NOTE {geni:about_me} Frank Julius Adler, 94, Administrative Director Emeritus of The Temple, Congregation B’nai Jehudah, died Friday, March 30, 2018 at Lawrence Memorial Hospital. Services will be held on Monday, April 2, 2018 at The T
2 CONC emple, Congregation B'nai Jehudah, starting with a musical prelude at 11:00 am. Burial will be in Rose Hill Cemetery. Memorial contributions may be made to the Frank J. Adler Leadership Endowment Fund of The Temple, Congregation B
2 CONC ’nai Jehudah, 12320 Nall Avenue, Overland Park, KS 66209, or to the Midwest Center for Holocaust Education, 5801 W. 115th Street, Overland Park, KS 66211.
2 CONT
2 CONT He was born as Franz Julius Schwarzadler in Frankfurt am Main, Germany, the city in which his father's family was the oldest one—non-Jewish as well as Jewish—in length of fully documented local residence: 14 successive generations
2 CONC , 1487–1939. He was a knowledgeable, observant fourth-generation Reform Jew. His high school education was begun at Frankfurt’s historic “Philanthropin” Reformrealgymnasium and completed in England after emigration following the “
2 CONC Kristallnacht” pogrom.
2 CONT
2 CONT He came to the United States (Chicago) at age 16 and immediately began full-time clerical employment. He received his college education in nine years of night classes before and following wartime U.S. Army service in New Guinea an
2 CONC d the Philippines, graduating from the University of Chicago’s College of Liberal Arts in 1952. He served as administrator of Temple Isaiah Israel (now K.A.M. Isaiah Israel), Chicago, for seven years (1946-1953), as well as its no
2 CONC n-ordained rabbinical assistant for six of those years. 
2 CONT
2 CONT Mr. Adler came to Kansas City in 1953 to become the administrative director of The Temple, Congregation B'nai Jehudah for the next thirty years. He then served B'nai Jehudah for an additional eight years as director of endowment d
2 CONC evelopment, retiring in 1991. Thereafter, for four more years, he was a Planned Giving consultant to other charities. 
2 CONT
2 CONT He was among the first of his profession to be certified as a Fellow in Temple Administration. He was the President of the National Association of Temple Administrators in 1963-65 and of the Mid-America Planned Giving Council in 1
2 CONC 989-1991. He served on the Board of Directors of the Midwest Center for Holocaust Education and on its speakers’ bureau. He was a member of the national Board of Trustees and Executive Committee of the Union of American Hebrew Con
2 CONC gregations (now Union for Reform Judaism) for four years and served as administrative consultant to other Midwest congregations for over two decades. He served as well on the board of the Jewish Community Relations Bureau of Great
2 CONC er Kansas City. He was a founding member of the National Association of Church Business Administrators’ Heart of America Chapter in 1971. He was an honorary member of B’nai Jehudah’s Sisterhood, as well as a member of the B’nai Je
2 CONC hudah Brotherhood, the Association of Reform Zionists of America, the World Union for Progressive Judaism, the Leo Baeck Institute, the U.S. Holocaust Memorial Council, and the Mainstream Coalition. 
2 CONT
2 CONT His centennial history volume of The Temple, Congregation B'nai Jehudah, Roots in a Moving Stream (1972), was praised nationwide as a model of excellence. He contributed numerous book reviews and articles on Jewish history and gen
2 CONC ealogy to The Kansas City Star, the Kansas City Jewish Chronicle, and to national and international journals. 
2 CONT
2 CONT The “Frank J. Adler Grove” of 1,000 trees in Israel’s Bicentennial National Forest near Jerusalem was dedicated by The Temple, Congregation B’nai Jehudah in 1978. He received the State of Israel’s “City of Peace Award” in 1984. B’
2 CONC nai Jehudah’s current administrative offices in Overland Park were designated as “The Frank J. Adler Administrative Suite.” 
2 CONT
2 CONT He was preceded in death by Lois Adler, his wife of 56 years. He is survived by his wife Elisabeth Adler; sons Daniel Adler (wife Jeanne), Leawood, and Dr. Paul Adler (wife Heidi), East Longmeadow, Mass; grandchildren Michelle Wex
2 CONC ler (husband David), David Adler (wife Allison), Jonathan Adler and Katherine Adler, as well as great-grandchildren Marissa Ann McLain and Jack Frank Adler.
2 CONT * Reference: [https://old.findagrave.com/cgi-bin/fg.cgi?page=gr&GRid=188410030 Find A Grave Memorial] - [http://www.geni.com/projects/SmartCopy/18783 SmartCopy]: ''Mar 30 2018, 19:46:18 UTC''
