3 CITY London
3 STAE England
3 CTRY United Kingdom
1 TITL Rabbi Dr.
1 NOTE {geni:about_me} Rabbi Dr. Hermann Adler CVO (30 May 1839 – 18 July 1911) was the Chief Rabbi of the British Empire from 1891 to 1911. The son (and successor as Chief Rabbi) of Nathan Marcus Adler, the 1911 Encyclopædia Britannica 
2 CONC writes that he "raised the position [of Chief Rabbi] to one of much dignity and importance."
2 CONT
2 CONT Born in Hanover, like his father, he had both a rabbinical education and a university education in Germany, and like him he subscribed to a modernised Orthodoxy. He attended University College School in London from 1852-54. He gra
2 CONC duated at Leipzig; he later received honorary degrees from Scottish and English universities, including Oxford.
2 CONT
2 CONT He was head of a congregation in Bayswater during his father's lifetime, and his father's assistant from the time his father's health began to deteriorate in 1879, before succeeding him on his death in 1891.
2 CONT
2 CONT In 1909 he was appointed a Commander of the Royal Victorian Order.
2 CONT
2 CONT Once he was having a lunch with British Catholic cardinal Herbert Vaughan. Cardinal asked the rabbi "Now, Dr. Adler, when may I have the pleasure of helping you to some ham?" The rabbi responded: "At Your Eminence's wedding".
2 CONT
2 CONT Adler wrote extensively on topics of Anglo-Jewish History and published two volumes of sermons. He was a vigorous defender of his co-religionists and their faith, as well as their sacred Scriptures.
2 CONT
2 CONT He is buried in the Willesden United Synagogue Cemetery, Beaconsfield Road NW10, London.
2 CONT
2 CONT Source: Wikipedia
2 CONT
2 CONT ----------
2 CONT
2 CONT http://www.jewisheastend.com/Willesden%20Cemetery%20Herman%20Adler.jpg
